I finished the subject (fairness) review for Ella Beatty and wrote `review-subject.json` to the run folder. It passes the schema check, and both hashes match the frozen draft and evidence. I changed no other files and published nothing.

**Verdict:** The draft handles the sensitive material well. The TMZ insinuation about Beatty and Sarah Paulson, the 2018 Page Six admissions item and her siblings are all left out, and there's no diagnosis-style language. It also qualifies as an open case: she's 26, her first role aired in January 2024 and her first lead in September 2026. The main fairness problem is selective quoting. The fear-first story keeps the "scared" half of answers whose other half is excitement or confidence.

**Major findings** (each blocks release until the editor fixes it or rejects it with a reason):
1. **"Fear is the emotion Beatty names most"** is the stated reason Type 6 leads, and the record doesn't support it. A rough word count across her saved interviews finds excitement, luck and love words about four times as often as fear words. Six of the eight video interviews contain no fear word at all.
2. **The "voracious" research quote** is used as proof she turns fear into work. In the source clip she frames it as excitement: "I was so excited... I felt very lucky." The opening also leaves out "I understand this person," from the same Variety answer as the "really scared" line.
3. **The Rebecca Hall "I did absolutely nothing" contrast** cuts off Hall's reason (she wanted creative freedom). It also drops Beatty's own "a little bit" in the same answer and ignores that Beatty is the lead while Hall and Hunnam are supporting. "The difference was hers, not the job's" doesn't follow.

**Six minor findings:**
- The ending sets a famous daughter beside a parent-killing role without her own warm account of her parents. Town & Country printed that account right after the same exchange.
- "Fell in love immediately" about Paulson is cut away from "one of my very best friends." Stripped of that frame, it's the line most easily used to feed the tabloid rumour.
- The "relief to come to work" and "very normal lady" quotes are used out of context.
- "It shows media sense more than motive" rules on her sincerity, which the record can't settle.
- A factual slip: "a new city." She had lived in New York since starting Juilliard in 2018.
- The type answer follows the same argument as the recent Grace Reiter, Lily-Rose Depp and Maleah Joi Moon drafts: a nervous newcomer who prepares hard and leans on trusted people. That's the reuse behind the similarity flag; the shared phrase itself is just ordinary type vocabulary.

**Podcast attribution checks out.** The draft's key podcast quotes came from a machine transcript, and no human had confirmed the speaker. I ran a pitch analysis on the audio. The "being where your feet are" passage and the other quoted answers are in a female voice (about 186–235 Hz); the male host sits around 120 Hz. That strongly supports crediting Beatty, though a human listen is still the gold standard.

I also kept five passages for the editor to preserve, including the opening scene at the Carlyle and the fair handling of whether her famous name helped her get cast. One limit: I checked quotes against the research stage's saved copies of each source (fetched today) rather than re-fetching the live pages.
